>
  <div class="prose-hgg mt-10">
    <h2>Start with the exact maintenance manual</h2>
    <p>Intervals and tasks differ by engine, controller, operating hours, environment, and manufacturer. The <a href="/maintenance/generator-maintenance-checklist/">maintenance checklist</a> is designed as a planning log, but the current manual for the exact generator controls the real service schedule.</p>
    <h2>Separate owner observations from technician work</h2>
    <p>Homeowners can usually observe status indicators, keep the surrounding area clear where the manual permits, note unusual noise or leakage, and keep service records. Oil/filter service, valve work, electrical diagnostics, transfer-equipment work, fuel-system work, and fault investigation may require qualified service depending on the equipment and task.</p>
    <h2>Build a readiness record</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Record exercise or self-test behavior and any alerts rather than assuming silence means readiness.</li>
      <li>Keep the exact model/serial number, current manuals, warranty documents, installer/service contact, and maintenance invoices together.</li>
      <li>Before severe-weather seasons, review access, debris, battery/service status, fuel availability, and any manufacturer cold-weather requirements.</li>
      <li>After a long outage or abnormal shutdown, follow the manufacturer’s inspection/service guidance before assuming the system is ready for the next event.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For climate-specific preparation, see the <a href="/guides/generator-climate-performance/">cold, heat and high-altitude planning guide</a>. For ownership budgeting, use the <a href="/cost/standby-generator-ownership-cost/">5- and 10-year cost worksheet</a>.</p>
  </div>
</CategoryLayout>
